  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM EDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nWidespread showers and storms are expected today as a shortwave \nmoves into the Tennessee Valley. A stationary boundary is currently \nover East and Middle Tennessee and will be lingering over the region \nor nearby today. Deep layer moisture advection is likely today with \nhigh pressure over the Atlantic bringing southerly surface winds and \na trough to the west bringing higher level moisture from the Gulf. \nNBM has high rain chances today with scattered showers and storms \nstarting in the morning hours and becoming widespread and numerous \nby early afternoon. CAMs have more isolated activity in the morning \nhours becoming scattered to numerous in the afternoon. \n\nA few strong to severe storms will be possible this afternoon with \ngood instability around 2000-2500 J/kg. With weak shear and high \nfreezing levels the primary threat will be gusty winds. With good \nmoisture advection, Pwat values will be elevated around 2 inches so \nheavy downpours will be likely at times that may cause some isolated \nflooding. Steering flow around 10-15 knots should help keep showers \nand storms moving, so overall the flooding threat is low.\n\nBy Sunday, the shortwave has moved east and a weak ridge builds into \nthe Lower Mississippi Valley. Therefore, showers and storms will be \nmore isolated on Sunday lingering to the east and northeast near the \nmountains. Showers may end completely by early or mid afternoon.\n\nEarly next week, a ridge will be over the Southeast. Monday looks \ndry with low chances of showers and storms returning Tuesday and \nWednesday in and near the east Tennessee mountains. Tennessee Valley \nlocations will remain dry. Thursday and Friday rain chances will be \nisolated in the Tennessee Valley and maybe scattered in and near the \nterrain.\n \nAfternoon highs will be above normal throughout the forecast period \nwith mainly upper 80s expected near Tri-Cities and lower 90s for \nKnoxville and Chattanooga areas, generally about 7 to 10 degrees \nabove normal. Today, Chattanooga broke the record for consecutive \ndays with a high temp of 90 or greater at 39 days. The streak may be \nbroken today with the forecast at 89 for Chattanooga since \nconvection may begin in the morning hours. If the timing ends up \nlater or coverage is not as widespread as expected, the streak may \ncontinue.",
